= Oct 13

== Satake isomorphism

Let $G = Spec A$ be a split connected reductive affine group scheme over $ZZ$. Let $T tilde.equiv GG_m^r$ be a split maximal torus, so that $cal(O)(T) = ZZ[X^*(T)]$ where
$
  X^* (T) = Hom(T, GG_m) tilde.equiv ZZ^r,\
  X_* (T) = Hom(GG_m, T) tilde.equiv ZZ^r.
$
There is the prefect pairing $chevron.l, chevron.r: X^* times X_* arrow ZZ$.
$B$ a Borel subgroup; $N$ the unipotent radical of $B$, $Phi$ the roots. $W$ the Wely group. $hat(G)$ the dual group. Chevalley restriction states that the restriction is an isomorphism:
$
  cal(O)(hat(G))^(hat(G)) arrow.tilde cal(O)(hat(T))^W.
$
$F$ is a non-archimedean local field, with $frak(O)$ its ring of integers, $pi.alt$ a uniformizer. It has residue field $FF_q$. $K = G(frak(O))$.

Define the valuation on $T$ by
$
  ord_T: T(F) arrow X_*(T), chevron lambda, ord_T (t) chevron.r = ord(lambda(t)) \
  Lambda colon.eq T(F) slash T(frak(O)) arrow.tilde X_*(T).
$
We write $pi.alt^lambda colon.eq lambda(pi.alt)$. Define the dominant elements to be
$
  Lambda^+ colon.eq {lambda: chevron alpha, lambda chevron.r >= 0, forall alpha in Phi^+}.
$
Note that every $W$-orbit in $Lambda$ meets $Lambda^+$ exactly once.

$G$ is unimodular, but $B$ is not, and the modulus character is
$
  delta(t n) = |det Ad_(Lie N) (t)| = |2 rho(t)|
$
where
$
  rho = 1/2 sum_(alpha in Phi^+) alpha in X^*(T) times.o QQ.
$
and
$
  rho(pi.alt^lambda) = q^(- chevron 2rho, lambda chevron.r).
$
There are the Iwasawa decomposition
$
  G(F) = B(F) G(frak(O)) = T(F)N(F)G(frak(O))
$
and the Cartan decomposition
$
  G(F) = union.sq.big_(lambda in Lambda^+) K pi.alt^lambda K.
$
$cal(H)(G(F), K)$ is the spherical Hecke algebra with unit $bold(1)_K$. It has basis
$
  phi_lambda = bold(1)_(K pi.alt^lambda K), quad lambda in Lambda^+.
$
In particular $cal(H)(T, T(frak(O))) = CC[Lambda]$ where
$
  bold(1)_(pi.alt^lambda T(frak(O))) * bold(1)_(pi.alt^mu T(frak(O))) = bold(1)_(pi.alt^(lambda + mu) T(frak(O)))
$
and
$
  cal(H)(T, T(frak(O))) = CC[X_*(T)] = cal(O)(hat(T)), quad bold(1)_(pi.alt^lambda T(frak(O))) arrow.l.r x^lambda.
$
Now define the Satake transform
$
  S f(t) = delta(t)^(1/2) integral_(N(f)) f(t n) dd n, quad f in cal(H)(G, K), t in T(F)
$
it is a function on $T(F)$, furthermore
$
  S f = sum_lambda S f(pi.alt^lambda) x^lambda in cal(O)(hat(T)).
$
There is also the counting form
$
  S f(pi.alt^lambda) = delta(pi.alt^lambda)^(1/2) dot \# {h K subset N(F) pi.alt^lambda K}
$
And this is in fact an isomorphism
$
  cal(H)(G, K) arrow.tilde cal(O)(hat(T))^W tilde.equiv cal(O)(hat(G))^(hat(G)).
$

In the case $G = GL_2 = Spec ZZ[x_11, x_12, x_21, x_22, det^(-1)]$, $X^*(T) = ZZ e_1 plus.o ZZ e_2$ and $X_*(T) = ZZ e_1^or plus.o ZZ e_2^or$. $Phi^+ = {alpha}, alpha = e_1 - e_2, alpha^or = e_1^or - e_2^or$. $rho = 1/2(e_1 - e_2)$. $W = S_2$ generated by $mat(0, 1; 1, 0)$.

$
  hat(T) = Spec CC[x_1^(plus.minus 1), x_2^(plus.minus 1)], quad x_i = x^(e_i^or)
$
and Chevalley restriction is given by
$
  cal(O)(hat(G))^(hat(G)) = CC[tr, det^(plus.minus 1)] arrow.tilde CC[x_1 + x_2, (x_1 x_2)^(plus.minus 1)] = cal(O)(hat(T))^abs((S_2))
$

For a dominant $mu = (mu_1, mu_2)$,
$
  chi_mu = det^(mu_2) dot tr Sym^(mu_1 - mu_2)
$
e.g.
$
  chi_((1, 0)) = x_1 + x_2 \
  chi_((1, 1)) = x_1 x_2 \
  chi_((2, 0)) = x_1^2 + x_1 x_2 + x_2^2
$

The Hecke algebra is spanned by
$
  t = phi_((1, 0)), s = quad phi_((1, 1))
$

Now for the Satake transform, the $K diag(pi.alt, 1) K$ has $q + 1$ cosets
$
  mat(pi.alt, beta; 0, 1), beta in frak(O) slash pi.alt frak(O), text("weight") q^(-1/2), quad mat(1, 0; 0, pi.alt), text("weight") q^(1/2)
$
so
$
  S phi_((1, 0)) = q^(1/2) (x_1 + x_2)
$
and similarly
$
  S phi_((1, 1)) = x_1 x_2.
$

== Principal series
$G = GL_2(F), K = GL_2(frak(O))$. For characters $chi_1, chi_2$ on $F^times$, $B$ the upper triangular Borel,
$
  I(chi_1, chi_2) = {f: G arrow CC text("locally constant"): f(mat(a, x; 0, d)g) = chi_1(a)chi_2(d)f(g)}.
$
When $chi_i$ not ramified, i.e. trivial on $frak(O)^times$, define $alpha_i = chi_i (pi.alt)$.

== When is a principal series spherical?

If both $chi_1$ and $chi_2$ unramified. For $f$ to be fixed by $K$ we need $f(b k) = chi(b) f(k) = chi(b)f(1)$. This $f(1)$ cannot be zero otherwise $f equiv 0$. Thus $chi$ must be trivial on $B(F) inter K$ and trivial on $frak(O)^times$.

== What is the Hecke module of $Ind_B^G (chi_1 times.o chi_2)$?
$cal(H)(G, K) = CC[t, s^(plus.minus 1)]$ acts on $I(chi_1, chi_2)^K$ by
$
  t mapsto q alpha_1 + alpha_2, quad s mapsto alpha_1 alpha_2
$

== What is $BC(Ind_B^G (chi_1 times.o chi_2))$ if the representation is spherical?

On the Galois side we have isomorphism classes of WD representations that are trivial on the inertia. This is determined by the matrix of the Frobenius up to conjugacy, i.e. its eigenvalues ${beta_1, beta_2}$.

On the automorphic side we have the irreducible representations of spherical $G$ representations, which is determined by its Hecke character.

LLC is given by sending ${beta_1, beta_2}$ to a unique irreducible representation $pi_F (beta_1, beta_2)$ with
$
  t mapsto q^(1/2) (beta_1 + beta_2), quad s mapsto beta_1 beta_2.
$
The left-hand-side restriction map is ${beta_1, beta_2} mapsto {beta_1^f, beta_2^f}$ since $Fr_(tilde(F)) = Fr_F^f$.

The right-hand-side base change map is
$
  pi_F (beta_1, beta_2) mapsto pi_(tilde(F)) (beta_1^f, beta_2^f).
$

To construct $pi_F$ we choose
$
  chi_1(pi.alt) = q^(-1/2) beta_1, quad chi_2(pi.alt) = q^(1/2) beta_2
$
then $I(chi_1, chi_2)$ has Hecke characters
$
  t mapsto q chi_1(pi.alt) + chi_2(pi.alt) = q^(1/2)(beta_1 + beta_2), quad s mapsto beta_1 beta_2
$
If $beta_1 slash beta_2 eq.not q^(plus.minus 1)$, $I(chi_1, chi_2)$ is irreducible and gives $pi_F$. Otherwise, $pi_F(beta_1, beta_2)$ is the $1$-dim subrepresentation $phi circle.small det$ inside $I(chi_1, chi_2)$.

== Infinite-dimensional spherical irreducible representations are principal series.

Let $lambda$ be the Hecke character. And $t' = lambda(t), s' = lambda(s)$ and let $alpha_1$ be a root of
$
  q X^2 - t'X + s
$
and $alpha_2 = s / alpha_1$. They are non-zero and satisfy
$
  q alpha_1 + alpha_2 = t', quad alpha_1 alpha_2 = s'
$
this gives the principal series.

== Local Langlands correspondence

- Decomposable, $N = 0, sigma = chi_1 plus.o chi_2, chi_1 chi_2^(-1) eq.not |dot|^(plus.minus)$: $I(chi_1 |dot|^(1/2), chi_2|dot|^(-1/2))$, i.e. irreducible principal series.

- Decomposable, $N = 0, sigma = phi |dot|^(1/2) plus.o phi|dot|^(-1/2)$: $phi circle.small det$.

- Indecomposable, $N eq.not 0, phi times.o sp(2)$, where $sp(2)$ is $|dot|^(-1/2) e_- plus.o |dot|^(1/2) e_+, N e_- = e_+, N e_+ = 0$: $phi St = I(phi, phi) slash phi circle.small det$.

- Irreducible $sigma = Ind_(W_E)^(W_F) xi$ for $(E slash F, xi)$ an admissible pair and $p eq.not 2$: supercuspidal c-$Ind_J^G Lambda_(Delta_xi xi)$

